From 2fb01a1adc57746e433d07e8639320d794e8cfff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ChenXiaoSong <chenxiaosong@kylinos.cn>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 16:03:06 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/7] smb/server: protect reparse handles from data operations

Prepare metadata-only handles for symbolic links. Reject data operations
on O_PATH and reparse handles, including truncation, allocation, zeroing,
copying and compression changes. Preserve the derived reparse attribute
when updating ordinary DOS attributes.

Allow directory emptiness checks to reopen an O_PATH handle using its
original credentials, and propagate errors from those checks. Symlink
opens are still disabled at this preparation stage.

Example (ordinary-file regression on a fresh writable test share):

  for mode in noposix posix; do
      mount -t cifs //${server_ip}/test /mnt \
          -o username=${smb_user},vers=3.1.1,$mode || exit 1
      printf 'abcdef\n' > /mnt/guard-test
      cat /mnt/guard-test
      truncate -s 3 /mnt/guard-test
      stat -c %s /mnt/guard-test
      cp /mnt/guard-test /mnt/guard-copy
      cmp /mnt/guard-test /mnt/guard-copy
      mv /mnt/guard-copy /mnt/guard-renamed
      rm /mnt/guard-test /mnt/guard-renamed
      mkdir /mnt/guard-dir
      rmdir /mnt/guard-dir
      umount /mnt || exit 1
  done

Expect abcdef from cat, 3 from stat, and all commands to succeed in both
modes. Enable SMB3 POSIX extensions on the server for the POSIX pass.
Once link opens are enabled, direct SMB data requests on such handles
must fail with STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED.

Code and test instructions generated with OpenAI Codex assistance.
